Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-26-2001||09:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierozilla:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by ray b:<BR>[b]what years have the alloy petal????<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>84-86 had the alum pedal. 87/88 had steel. (I believe)<P>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>They changed over to the steel pedal in mid '86.  I have a later '86 and have the steel pedal.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||10-26-2001||10:25 PM||||I have some pictures of a good pedal and a bad pedal in this thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014590.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/014590.html</A> <P>I'm posting those pics again here because this is very important.<P>The problem we have now is not the pin size. Design #1 has not been available since about 1986. It was Aluminium and had the large pin. Design #2 was the steel pedal and it had a small pin. Pin sizes really don't matter because you can make the the small pin on the newer pedals fit the old banjo by just running over to the hardware store and buying a 79 cent bushing. You can make the OLDER large dia. pin fit the newer small dia. banjo by just removing the existing bushing in the banjo.<P>There were no Steel pedals that were made with a large Diameter pin. They were in some old version P-22 manuals but I've never seen one. So the only people who would need to change from the large pin to the small pin would be those who have an Aluminium pedal he is trying to use with a new style Master Cylinder.<P>The real problem is that the Alum. pedal bends and the steel pedal doesn't. <P>This is the AL pedal...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Alum_1.jpg"> <BR>See how the bracket on the left is is bent to make the pin closer to the piviot point of the pedal.<P>Compare it to this steel pedal...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/steel_1.jpg"> <P>As the "U" shaped bracket on the Aluminium pedal bends it brings the "banjo" pin up closer to the piviot point of the pedal. This shortens the amount of travel of the Banjo as the pedal is pushed down. As this bracket comes under load as the pedal is pushed down it bends the bracket even more than you see here. The more the bracket bends the closer the pin moves toward the pedal pivot point. The closer the pin moves toward the pedal pivot point the less travel you get out of the banjo. This "Catch 22" situation leads to the transmission grinding as you try to shift into gear and progresses until you waste the clutch.<P>You know.... heck... I wasn't going to make this a lecture but I might as well finish what I have to say on this subject.<P>I know this will sound like I'm rambling on but please try to follow me here.....<P>Many people know that I've been advocating the replacement of the Al. pedals with steel ones for many years.... in fact I've been telling people about this steel pedal replacement for more than 13 years.  <P>Back in early 1987 a friend of mine (Frank Whiton, now a Hydraulics Engineer on the Viper and Prowler braking systems for MB) and myself investigated the Fiero Clutch system. We found the flaws in the clutch pedal at that time. <P>Now if you remember the early Fieros had a lot of clutch failures while they were still under new car warrantee. These people would take their Fiero to the dealer and thinking it would be under warrantee would let the dealer R&R the clutch. MANY... MANY of them would then be told that their failure was not covered under warrantee because they "Had been riding the clutch". With the bent bracket as I described earlier, the repair guy who had to fix the failed clutch would think that it failed because of riding the clutch. The bent pedal would make the bad clutch appear to have failed due to riding the clutch so the Warrantee wouldn't apply.<P>Another thing we discovered was that in late 1986 (when they released the 2nd. design steel pedal) Pontiac also released an internal service memo to Fiero Techs. where-in they were instructed to replace the pedal on ANY & ALL Fieros that were brought in for any service including clutch repair. They were further instructed to not charge the customer for this replacement and to not note the replacement on the customer invoice. I have a copy of this memo someplace in my archives.<P>Anyway...... Pontiac knew of this problem that Frank and I had discovered on our own. And Pontiac also knew that if the thousands of customers whom had had to pay for clutch repair, knew about the clutch pedal thing, there would have been a class action suite and Pontiac would have had to pay for a lot of clutch R&R's.<P>I believe to this day that one of the contributing reasons Pontiac discontinued the Fiero was that they wanted to limit thier potential liability on this issue. <P>By the time Frank and I finally figured out that Pontiac didn't want to hear about this problem, they had already announced the shut down of Fiero production.<P>If we would have known then what we know now & if there was someway to communicate with thousands of Fiero owners (like there is now with the advent of the Internet) we probally should have proceeded with some kind of lawsuit.<P>That's the way it happened and wether you believe it or not, 99% of the original Fiero clutch problems were caused by that stupid Suckin' pedal design.<P>WAKE-UP.....the problem is not the pin....it's the PEDAL.<P>Archie<P>Sorry about the typo's.... I had to type this in a hurry..... because I gotta post up Day #2 on another thread yet tonight.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodney||10-26-2001||11:07 PM||||The problem we have now is not the pin size. Design #1 has not been available since about 1986. It was Aluminium and had the large pin. Design #2 was the steel pedal and it had a small pin.<P>--------------------------------------<P>I have 2 of them here. Steel and large pin. Got them both from Ed Parks. I consider these the #2 design. The #3 design is the steel with small pin. Possibly they did not make too many of the #2 design before they went to the #3 design? Possibly the #2 design is late 86 only? ||||reg||1
